1
00:02:42,857 --> 00:02:48,857
The Lost Pharaoh: The Search for Akhenaten is a
documentary film directed by Nicholas Kendall.

2
00:05:25,714 --> 00:05:31,714
Ancient pharaoh Akhenaten was almost lost to history.

3
00:08:08,571 --> 00:08:14,571
Canadian archaeologist Donald Redford, who uncovered the foundation of one of the pharaoh’s many
temples, attempts to finally piece together this great Egyptian ruler’s enigmatic story.

4
00:10:51,428 --> 00:10:57,428
Self - Narrator (voice), played by Donald Brittain.

5
00:13:34,285 --> 00:13:40,285
Akhenaten (pronounced  ), also spelled Akhenaton or Echnaton (Ancient Egyptian: ꜣḫ-n-jtn ʾŪḫə-nə-yātəy, pronounced
[ˈʔuːχəʔ nə ˈjaːtəj] , meaning 'Effective for the Aten'), was an ancient Egyptian pharaoh reigning c.

6
00:16:17,142 --> 00:16:23,142
1353–1336 or 1351–1334 BC, the tenth ruler of the Eighteenth Dynasty.

7
00:19:00,000 --> 00:19:06,000
Originally named Amenhotep IV  (Ancient Egyptian: jmn-ḥtp, meaning "Amun is satisfied", Hellenized
as Amenophis IV), in the fifth year of his reign he adopted the name "Akhenaten".

8
00:21:42,857 --> 00:21:48,857
As a pharaoh, Akhenaten is noted for abandoning traditional ancient Egyptian religion
of polytheism and introducing Atenism, or worship centered around Aten.

9
00:24:25,714 --> 00:24:31,714
The views of Egyptologists differ as to whether the religious policy was absolutely
monotheistic, or whether it was monolatristic, syncretistic, or henotheistic.

10
00:27:08,571 --> 00:27:14,571
This culture shift away from traditional religion was reversed after his death.

11
00:29:51,428 --> 00:29:57,428
Akhenaten's monuments were dismantled and hidden, his statues were destroyed, and
his name excluded from lists of rulers compiled by later pharaohs.

12
00:32:34,285 --> 00:32:40,285
Traditional religious practice was gradually restored, notably under his close successor
Tutankhamun, who changed his name from Tutankhaten early in his reign.

13
00:35:17,142 --> 00:35:23,142
When, some dozen years later, rulers without clear rights of succession from the Eighteenth Dynasty founded a new dynasty, they
discredited Akhenaten and his immediate successors and referred to Akhenaten as "the enemy" or "that criminal" in archival records.

14
00:38:00,000 --> 00:38:06,000
Akhenaten was all but lost to history until the late-19th-century discovery of Amarna,
or Akhetaten, the new capital city he built for the worship of Aten.

15
00:40:42,857 --> 00:40:48,857
Furthermore, in 1907 a mummy that could be Akhenaten's was unearthed
from the tomb KV55 in the Valley of the Kings by Edward R.

16
00:43:25,714 --> 00:43:31,714
Genetic testing has determined that the man buried in KV55 was Tutankhamun's
father, but its identification as Akhenaten has since been questioned.

17
00:46:08,571 --> 00:46:14,571
Akhenaten's rediscovery and Flinders Petrie's early excavations at Amarna
sparked great public interest in the pharaoh and his queen Nefertiti.

18
00:48:51,428 --> 00:48:57,428
He has been described as "enigmatic", "mysterious", "revolutionary", "the greatest idealist of the world", and
"the first individual in history", but also as a "heretic", "fanatic", "possibly insane", and "mad".

19
00:51:34,285 --> 00:51:40,285
Public and scholarly fascination with Akhenaten comes from his connection with Tutankhamun, the unique style and high quality
of the pictorial arts he patronized, and the religion he attempted to establish, foreshadowing monotheism.

20
00:54:17,142 --> 00:54:23,142
The film explores themes of ancient history, ancient egypt, archaeology.
